    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "Each of the numbers in the list is called a term.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b14",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      352.29,
      420.13,
      390.95
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "Given a term, can you write the next term in each of the lists above? If so, how\nwill you write it? Perhaps by following a pattern or rule. Let us observe and write the\nrule.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b15",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      391.89,
      306.78,
      404.15
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "In (i), each term is 1 more than the term preceding it.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b16",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      409.18,
      308.93,
      421.43
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "In (ii), each term is 30 less than the term preceding it.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b17",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      426.46,
      363.87,
      438.71
    ],
    "reading_order": 17,
    "text": "In (iii), each term is obtained by adding 1 to  the term preceding it.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b18",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      443.5,
      420.12,
      468.95
    ],
    "reading_order": 18,
    "text": "In (iv), all the terms in the list are 3 , i.e., each term is obtained by adding\n(or subtracting) 0 to the term preceding it.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b19",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      473.02,
      420.07,
      498.47
    ],
    "reading_order": 19,
    "text": "In (v), each term is obtained by adding – 0.5 to (i.e., subtracting 0.5 from) the\nterm preceding it.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b20",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      502.3,
      420.01,
      540.96
    ],
    "reading_order": 20,
    "text": "In all the lists above, we see that successive terms are obtained by adding a fixed\nnumber to the preceding terms. Such list of numbers is said to form an  Arithmetic\nProgression ( AP ).",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b21",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      544.61,
      420.0,
      583.68
    ],
    "reading_order": 21,
    "text": "So, an arithmetic progression is a list of numbers in which each term  is\nobtained by adding a fixed number to the preceding term except the first\nterm.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 3,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p003_b22",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.01,
      587.09,
      420.06,
      612.96
    ],
    "reading_order": 22,
    "text": "This fixed number is called the common difference of the AP. Remember that\nit can be positive, negative or zero.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
